 # Registry Design
 
-## Context
+## Context and Design Philosophy
 
 Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
 
+Kiosks are offline, so the registry must resolve lookups entirely from local files and rebuild its search state from USB-delivered snapshots. All snapshot validation, index maintenance, and lookup belong to this node; the certificates node sees only the lookup interface.
+
 ## Architecture
 
 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index, private to this node. Each kiosk holds one active index file built from the most recent valid snapshot. Importing a snapshot means validating it, building a fresh index file from its records, and atomically switching the active index to the new file. Lookup is a parameterized query against the active index file; no other component reads SQLite storage.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the replacement SQLite index in a staging file beside the active one; the active index is never modified in place. While the import runs, lookups continue against the active index. After all validation and index building succeed, activate the staged index with an atomic file swap. An import failure at any point discards the staging file, leaves the previous index active, and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Normalized serials are stored in an indexed column so a lookup is an indexed point query, meeting the 250 ms goal on 90,000 records. Return the snapshot date — read from the active index's metadata — even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index rebuilt per kiosk from each snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory service; managed search service | No runtime network access, which the HLD tenets require; predictable indexed point queries meet the 250 ms goal at 90,000 records; no recurring cost, credentials, or service to operate. Trade-off: each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index per snapshot, and that rebuild cost is contained inside this node. The HTTPS option made lookups depend on the lab network and added timeout handling; the managed service added cost, credentials, and external connectivity. |
+| Index activation | Build a staging index file, then atomically swap it in after validation succeeds | In-place index updates during import | Keeps a failed import from corrupting the last valid snapshot (HLD tenet) and lets lookups continue uninterrupted during an import. |
+| Lookup performance | Store normalized serials in an indexed SQLite column | Scanning records per query | An indexed point query holds the 250 ms goal at 90,000 records without depending on machine speed headroom. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Lookup backend selection: local embedded SQLite index (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+1. First-boot behavior: what a kiosk with no imported snapshot yet returns for a lookup (empty result shape, and whether a snapshot date exists to report).
+2. Staging-file disk budget: whether a size cap or cleanup policy is needed for stale staging files after crashes mid-import.
